.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/d3-path Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "d3-path"
.PP
Say you have some code that draws to a 2D canvas:

.PP
.PP
.nf
function drawCircle(context, radius) {
  context\&.moveTo(radius, 0);
  context\&.arc(0, 0, radius, 0, 2 * Math\&.PI);
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
The d3-path module lets you take this exact code and additionally render to \fRSVG\fP\&. It works by \fBserializing\fP \fRCanvasPathMethods\fP calls to \fRSVG path data\fP\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const context = d3\&.path();
drawCircle(context, 40);
pathElement\&.setAttribute("d", context\&.toString());
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now code you write once can be used with both Canvas (for performance) and SVG (for convenience)\&. For a practical example, see \fRd3-shape\fP\&.
.SH "Installing"
.PP
If you use npm, \fRnpm install d3-path\fP\&. You can also download the \fRlatest release on GitHub\fP\&. In modern browsers, you can import d3-path from jsDelivr:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script type="module">

import {path} from "https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-path@3/+esm";

const p = path();
p\&.moveTo(1, 2);
p\&.lineTo(3, 4);
p\&.closePath();

</script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
For legacy environments, you can load d3-path’s UMD bundle from an npm-based CDN such as jsDelivr; a \fRd3\fP global is exported:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-path@3"></script>
<script>

const path = d3\&.path();

</script>
.fi
.PP
.SH "API Reference"
.PP
# d3\&.\fBpath\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new path serializer that implements \fRCanvasPathMethods\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBmoveTo\fP(\fIx\fP, \fIy\fP)

.PP
Move to the specified point ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.moveTo](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-moveto) and SVG’s \fR“moveto” command\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBclosePath\fP()

.PP
Ends the current subpath and causes an automatic straight line to be drawn from the current point to the initial point of the current subpath\&. Equivalent to \fR\fIcontext\fP\&.closePath\fP and SVG’s \fR“closepath” command\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBlineTo\fP(\fIx\fP, \fIy\fP)

.PP
Draws a straight line from the current point to the specified point ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.lineTo](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-lineto) and SVG’s \fR“lineto” command\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBquadraticCurveTo\fP(\fIcpx\fP, \fIcpy\fP, \fIx\fP, \fIy\fP)

.PP
Draws a quadratic Bézier segment from the current point to the specified point ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩, with the specified control point ⟨*cpx\fP, \fIcpy*⟩\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.quadraticCurveTo](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-quadraticcurveto) and SVG’s \fRquadratic Bézier curve commands\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBbezierCurveTo\fP(\fIcpx1\fP, \fIcpy1\fP, \fIcpx2\fP, \fIcpy2\fP, \fIx\fP, \fIy\fP)

.PP
Draws a cubic Bézier segment from the current point to the specified point ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩, with the specified control points ⟨*cpx1\fP, \fIcpy1*⟩ and ⟨*cpx2\fP, \fIcpy2*⟩\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.bezierCurveTo](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-beziercurveto) and SVG’s \fRcubic Bézier curve commands\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBarcTo\fP(\fIx1\fP, \fIy1\fP, \fIx2\fP, \fIy2\fP, \fIradius\fP)

.PP
Draws a circular arc segment with the specified \fIradius\fP that starts tangent to the line between the current point and the specified point ⟨*x1*, \fIy1*⟩ and ends tangent to the line between the specified points ⟨*x1\fP, \fIy1*⟩ and ⟨*x2\fP, \fIy2*⟩\&. If the first tangent point is not equal to the current point, a straight line is drawn between the current point and the first tangent point\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.arcTo](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-arcto) and uses SVG’s \fRelliptical arc curve commands\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBarc\fP(\fIx\fP, \fIy\fP, \fIradius\fP, \fIstartAngle\fP, \fIendAngle\fP[, \fIanticlockwise\fP])

.PP
Draws a circular arc segment with the specified center ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩, *radius\fP, \fIstartAngle\fP and \fIendAngle\fP\&. If \fIanticlockwise\fP is true, the arc is drawn in the anticlockwise direction; otherwise, it is drawn in the clockwise direction\&. If the current point is not equal to the starting point of the arc, a straight line is drawn from the current point to the start of the arc\&. Equivalent to \fR\fIcontext\fP\&.arc\fP and uses SVG’s \fRelliptical arc curve commands\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBrect\fP(\fIx\fP, \fIy\fP, \fIw\fP, \fIh\fP)

.PP
Creates a new subpath containing just the four points ⟨*x*, \fIy*⟩, ⟨*x\fP + \fIw\fP, \fIy*⟩, ⟨*x\fP + \fIw\fP, \fIy\fP + \fIh*⟩, ⟨*x\fP, \fIy\fP + \fIh*⟩, with those four points connected by straight lines, and then marks the subpath as closed\&. Equivalent to [*context\fP\&.rect](http://www.w3.org/TR/2dcontext/#dom-context-2d-rect) and uses SVG’s \fR“lineto” commands\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpath\fP\&.\fBtoString\fP()

.PP
Returns the string representation of this \fIpath\fP according to SVG’s \fRpath data specification\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBpathRound\fP(\fIdigits\fP = 3) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Like \fBd3\&.path\fP, except limits the digits after the decimal to the specified number of \fIdigits\fP\&. 
